● Root Cause Analysis Complete:

  The issue was improper weight initialization causing numerical explosion:
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │                Original Pattern                │             Problem             │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ w1[i] = 0.001f * (((i % 100) - 50) / 50.0f)    │ Cyclic pattern [-0.001, +0.001] │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Combined with input pattern (i % 256) / 255.0f │ Creates resonance during matmul │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Result                                         │ Values explode to 10^33         │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘
  Fix: Use Xavier-like weight initialization scaling:
  float w1_scale = std::sqrt(2.0f / (fan_in + fan_out));
  w1[i] = pseudo_random * w1_scale;

  Results now correct:
  - MNIST MLP Output[0]: 0.024498 (reasonable)
  - Transformer FFN Output[0]: 0.002359 (unchanged)
